president biden announcing new efforts  to curb the recent rise in violent crime  across our country  as washington correspondent jessie  trunoa reports his plan focuses on  addressing gun violence providing money  to cities that need more police  and offering community support  [Music]  president joe biden is taking aim at  rising gun violence across the country  with a new federal strategy the  president's plan cracks down on gun  dealers who break federal law  and create strike forces in several  major cities to help stop weapons  trafficking  it also encourages local leaders to  invest covet 19 relief funds  into policing it's not just hiring  police officers but it's also  making sure there's funding so that they  are getting trained that they have the  tools needed  to deal with the community in a safe way  and accountable way  to continue that community approach  white house principal deputy press  secretary karine jean-pierre  says the plan also suggests cities  prioritize summer jobs for teenagers  and re-entry options for formerly  incarcerated americans  working closely with them to make sure  that they have a safe summer  chicago is the uh the largest outdoor  shooting range in the world right now  republicans like louisiana senator john  kennedy and tennessee senator marsha  blackburn  argue police departments can't fight  crime if they're also under attack  they point to efforts in some cities to  cut police budgets  in favor of mental health services  housing assistance  and other programs it has demoralized  police forces and it has  emboldened criminal activity  and this is something that has to stop  but jean-pierre says  that's an empty argument the president  has been very clear he does not believe  in defunding the police  in washington i'm jesse tannor
 

